1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,608
The Chinese Protectorate: Pickering,

2
00:00:02,688 --> 00:00:05,525
the Poh Leung Kuk and the Coolie Trade

3
00:00:05,525 --> 00:00:09,944
On the 18th of July 1887, a
Teochew carpenter named Chua

4
00:00:10,024 --> 00:00:13,961
Ah Siok walked into the
office of the Protector of

5
00:00:14,041 --> 00:00:18,700
Chinese in Singapore, saying
he had a petition to present.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,841
When he reached the
Protector, William Pickering,

7
00:00:21,921 --> 00:00:24,550
he threw the iron head of an axe at him.

8
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:29,025
It struck Pickering on the forehead,
and he never fully recovered.

9
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:32,487
The attack was blamed on a
secret society, and it was

10
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:35,695
aimed at the man who, for
ten years, had run the

11
00:00:35,775 --> 00:00:38,969
colony's first serious
attempt to protect Chinese

12
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:42,725
migrants from the trade that
brought them to Singapore.

13
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:47,344
Through the nineteenth century,
most of Singapore's growth came

14
00:00:47,424 --> 00:00:51,819
from Chinese migrants, and by
the 1870s about 50,000 of them

15
00:00:51,899 --> 00:00:56,300
were arriving every year from
Fujian, Guangdong and Hainan.

16
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,625
Many came under the credit-ticket system.

17
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:03,491
A broker paid the passage of
a man who could not afford it,

18
00:01:03,571 --> 00:01:06,635
and on arrival the
newcomer, known as a sinkeh,

19
00:01:06,715 --> 00:01:09,725
had to sign a contract
and work off the debt.

20
00:01:09,725 --> 00:01:12,800
Until a broker or employer
took the debt over,

21
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:16,709
he could be kept in the ship's
hold or in a coolie depot,

22
00:01:16,789 --> 00:01:20,150
and the depots were often
overcrowded and filthy.

23
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:23,384
The system was open to
abuse, and much of the

24
00:01:23,464 --> 00:01:26,925
trade was in the hands
of the secret societies.

25
00:01:26,925 --> 00:01:31,101
In 1873 three leading Chinese
merchants, Hoo Ah Kay Whampoa,

26
00:01:31,181 --> 00:01:35,995
Tan Kim Ching and Tan Seng Poh, told
the Chamber of Commerce that the

27
00:01:36,075 --> 00:01:40,747
head of the Ghee Hok society sent
boats out to the immigrant ships,

28
00:01:40,827 --> 00:01:45,225
supposedly to protect the coolies
but in reality to sell them.

29
00:01:45,225 --> 00:01:48,522
Men who thought they were
going to work in nearby Johor

30
00:01:48,602 --> 00:01:51,898
or Riau found themselves
shipped to the tobacco estates

31
00:01:51,978 --> 00:01:55,459
of Deli in Sumatra, where a
coolie fetched a higher price,

32
00:01:55,539 --> 00:01:58,425
and some killed themselves
when they found out.

33
00:01:58,425 --> 00:02:03,184
The Chinese merchants had already
petitioned the government in 1871,

34
00:02:03,264 --> 00:02:06,173
asking for an immigration office where the

35
00:02:06,253 --> 00:02:10,025
coolies would be landed and
told that they were free.

36
00:02:10,025 --> 00:02:13,352
William Alexander Pickering was born in

37
00:02:13,432 --> 00:02:17,450
Nottinghamshire in 1840
and went to sea at 16.

38
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:21,841
In 1862 he joined the Chinese
customs service at Fuzhou,

39
00:02:21,921 --> 00:02:26,152
and he went on to work in
Taiwan, then called Formosa,

40
00:02:26,232 --> 00:02:29,425
learning to speak Chinese along the way.

41
00:02:29,425 --> 00:02:35,194
In March 1872 he arrived in Singapore
as the colony's Chinese interpreter,

42
00:02:35,274 --> 00:02:38,356
and at the time he was the only European

43
00:02:38,436 --> 00:02:43,100
officer in the Straits Settlements
who could speak Chinese.

44
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,608
He came to speak Mandarin,
Hokkien, Teochew,

45
00:02:46,688 --> 00:02:51,174
Cantonese and Hakka, and he
helped settle the Larut wars

46
00:02:51,254 --> 00:02:55,250
between rival Chinese
societies in Perak in 1874.

47
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:59,526
On the 3rd of May 1877 Pickering
was appointed the first

48
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:04,503
Protector of Chinese, at the head
of a new Chinese Protectorate,

49
00:03:04,583 --> 00:03:09,250
and later that year he was also
made Registrar of Societies.

50
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:12,886
The same year, the colony
passed a Chinese Immigrants

51
00:03:12,966 --> 00:03:16,110
Ordinance and a Crimping
Ordinance to regulate

52
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:19,275
how labourers were
brought in and recruited.

53
00:03:19,275 --> 00:03:23,240
The Protectorate began in a
shophouse on North Canal Road,

54
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:28,550
with an Assistant Protector, a boarding
officer, an interpreter and clerks.

55
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:33,550
Its officer boarded every ship
that brought in Chinese migrants.

56
00:03:33,550 --> 00:03:36,982
Each sinkeh was given a
handbill telling him that the

57
00:03:37,062 --> 00:03:41,820
Protectorate would help him and warning
him against the secret societies,

58
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:45,200
and each contract between
a sinkeh and his employer

59
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:48,925
was registered and explained
to him in his own dialect.

60
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:53,225
The coolie brokers and their
depots had to be licensed.

61
00:03:53,225 --> 00:03:55,043
With the secret societies,

62
00:03:55,123 --> 00:03:59,575
Pickering preferred registration
and close watching to a ban.

63
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:03,179
As Registrar he kept records
of each society's address,

64
00:04:03,259 --> 00:04:06,996
office-bearers and members,
worked through their headmen,

65
00:04:07,076 --> 00:04:10,425
and had the ones who would
not cooperate banished.

66
00:04:10,425 --> 00:04:14,394
Over time, Chinese residents
began to bring their disputes

67
00:04:14,474 --> 00:04:17,744
to the Protectorate instead
of to the societies,

68
00:04:17,824 --> 00:04:22,850
and grateful petitioners lined the
walls of his office with red candles.

69
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:26,500
The Protectorate soon
outgrew its first office.

70
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,827
It moved to Upper Macao Street,
which is now named Pickering

71
00:04:30,907 --> 00:04:35,013
Street after him, then to Boat
Quay, and in 1886 to a new

72
00:04:35,093 --> 00:04:39,500
building at the corner of New
Bridge Road and Havelock Road.

73
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,775
The migrants were overwhelmingly men.

74
00:04:42,775 --> 00:04:47,610
In 1860 there was about one
Chinese woman in Singapore for

75
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:53,965
every 14 men, and by 1882 at least
2,000 women were working as prostitutes,

76
00:04:54,045 --> 00:04:57,350
about a third of the female population.

77
00:04:57,350 --> 00:05:01,539
Prostitution itself was legal,
but the Protectorate registered

78
00:05:01,619 --> 00:05:05,327
the brothels and the women
in them, and it could remove

79
00:05:05,407 --> 00:05:09,125
any girl under 16 who was
being trained for the trade.

80
00:05:09,125 --> 00:05:12,221
A similar refuge, the Po Leung Kuk,

81
00:05:12,301 --> 00:05:15,750
had been founded in Hong Kong in 1878.

82
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:19,699
In Singapore, to give the
women and girls it rescued

83
00:05:19,779 --> 00:05:23,341
somewhere to go, a
committee of leading Chinese

84
00:05:23,421 --> 00:05:27,450
residents began meeting
under the Protector in 1886.

85
00:05:27,450 --> 00:05:31,708
In 1888 the Women and Girls'
Protection Ordinance made

86
00:05:31,787 --> 00:05:36,125
Singapore's Poh Leung Kuk
an official place of safety.

87
00:05:36,125 --> 00:05:39,809
It took in rescued prostitutes,
mui tsai (girls sold as

88
00:05:39,889 --> 00:05:43,504
domestic servants) and
destitute girls, taught them to

89
00:05:43,584 --> 00:05:48,158
read and to keep house, and at 18
helped them into work or marriage,

90
00:05:48,238 --> 00:05:50,975
vetting the men who asked to marry them.

91
00:05:50,975 --> 00:05:56,775
It probably started in a single room
at the Lock Hospital at Kandang Kerbau,

92
00:05:56,855 --> 00:06:00,800
and in 1928 it moved to a
larger home at York Hill.

93
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:04,487
A League of Nations committee
that visited in 1931

94
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:07,878
called it the finest
institution of its kind,

95
00:06:07,958 --> 00:06:11,575
though critics saw it as
too much like a prison.

96
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:17,355
Pickering's regulations were resented
by members of the secret societies,

97
00:06:17,435 --> 00:06:20,325
and in 1887 one of them struck back.

98
00:06:20,325 --> 00:06:25,368
According to Song Ong Siang's
history of the Chinese in Singapore,

99
00:06:25,448 --> 00:06:28,473
published in 1923, Chua Ah Siok had been

100
00:06:28,553 --> 00:06:32,900
put up to the attack by members
of the Ghee Hok society.

101
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:37,054
He was arrested on the spot, and
said that although he had been

102
00:06:37,134 --> 00:06:41,100
hired to kill, he had decided
not to kill a public officer.

103
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,737
At the November 1887
assizes he pleaded guilty

104
00:06:44,817 --> 00:06:48,292
to voluntarily causing
grievous hurt and was

105
00:06:48,372 --> 00:06:52,250
sentenced to seven years'
rigorous imprisonment.

106
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:56,100
Pickering never fully
recovered from the wound.

107
00:06:56,100 --> 00:07:00,092
He went on extended leave
and retired by 1890;

108
00:07:00,172 --> 00:07:03,625
the sources disagree on the exact year.

109
00:07:03,625 --> 00:07:08,470
The new governor, Sir Cecil
Clementi Smith, chose a harder line,

110
00:07:08,550 --> 00:07:11,702
and under the Societies Ordinance of 1889,

111
00:07:11,782 --> 00:07:17,400
which came into force in 1890, the
secret societies were banned outright.

112
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:22,775
Pickering died at San Remo
in Italy in January 1907.

113
00:07:22,775 --> 00:07:27,625
The Protectorate outlived its
founder by more than half a century.

114
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:30,699
Its later heads included Francis Powell,

115
00:07:30,779 --> 00:07:33,775
George Crofton Wray and William Evans.

116
00:07:33,775 --> 00:07:40,066
In 1928 work began on a new building
on the site of the 1886 office,

117
00:07:40,146 --> 00:07:44,550
and the Protectorate
moved into it around 1930.

118
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:49,158
It is a two-storey neoclassical
building with Art Deco details,

119
00:07:49,238 --> 00:07:54,000
in a style close to that of the
old City Hall and Supreme Court.

120
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,045
After the Second World War
the Protectorate was renamed

121
00:07:58,125 --> 00:08:02,245
the Chinese Secretariat and
placed under the Ministry of

122
00:08:02,325 --> 00:08:06,675
Labour and Welfare, which moved
into the building in 1955.

123
00:08:06,675 --> 00:08:10,732
The Poh Leung Kuk did not
survive the war in its old form:

124
00:08:10,812 --> 00:08:14,297
it was left out of the
plans of the new Department

125
00:08:14,377 --> 00:08:18,300
of Social Welfare, and its
committee quietly dissolved.

126
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:21,724
The building later housed
the Ministry of Law,

127
00:08:21,804 --> 00:08:25,457
and from 1990 the courts,
first as the Family and

128
00:08:25,537 --> 00:08:29,650
Juvenile Court and later as
the Family Justice Courts.

129
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:33,031
On the 27th of February 1998 it was

130
00:08:33,111 --> 00:08:36,275
gazetted as a national monument.

131
00:08:36,275 --> 00:08:39,777
In November 2024 the Family Justice Courts

132
00:08:39,857 --> 00:08:43,950
moved to new premises
nearby at Havelock Square.

133
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:48,075
Some of the Protectorate's
work has present-day echoes.

134
00:08:48,075 --> 00:08:51,830
Singapore's Prevention of
Human Trafficking Act took

135
00:08:51,910 --> 00:08:57,583
effect on the 1st of March 2015, and an
inter-agency taskforce on trafficking,

136
00:08:57,663 --> 00:09:00,238
set up in 2010, is co-chaired by the

137
00:09:00,318 --> 00:09:04,300
Ministry of Home Affairs and
the Ministry of Manpower.

138
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:08,543
Since 2018 the Ministry of
Manpower has required newly arrived

139
00:09:08,623 --> 00:09:13,981
work permit holders in some sectors to
attend a one-day Settling-In Programme,

140
00:09:14,061 --> 00:09:16,073
taught in their own languages,

141
00:09:16,153 --> 00:09:20,814
which explains their rights,
Singapore's laws and where to get help,

142
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:25,775
much as Pickering's handbills tried
to do for the sinkeh of the 1870s.

143
00:09:25,775 --> 00:09:30,010
Where it fits in the bigger story:
The Chinese Protectorate was the

144
00:09:30,090 --> 00:09:34,196
colonial government's first real
attempt to stand between Chinese

145
00:09:34,276 --> 00:09:38,190
migrants and the brokers and
societies who profited from them,

146
00:09:38,270 --> 00:09:43,100
and it was pushed for by Chinese
merchants as much as by British officials.

147
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:46,931
It did not end the abuses, and
its handbills and registers

148
00:09:47,011 --> 00:09:50,708
reached only part of the
trade, but it brought migrants,

149
00:09:50,788 --> 00:09:54,025
women and girls under the
protection of the law.

150
00:09:54,025 --> 00:09:58,352
The building at New Bridge Road and
Havelock Road is still standing,

151
00:09:58,432 --> 00:10:00,555
and Pickering Street, a short lane

152
00:10:00,635 --> 00:10:03,875
in the middle of the city,
still carries his name.
